# P3.4 fix — use the ifupdown PACKAGE's /sbin/ifup{,down} (stock parity) instead
# of busybox's applets. Stock ships the real ifupdown package (usr/sbin/ifup is a
# libc-linked ELF); busybox's ifup applet mishandles the /etc/network/interfaces
# pre-up "$IFACE" substitution, so wpa_supplicant is invoked with malformed args
# and dumps its full usage text (twice, once per wlanN stanza) at every boot.
# Disabling the busybox applets lets BR2_PACKAGE_IFUPDOWN's binaries win (with
# BR2_ROOTFS_MERGED_USR, /sbin/ifup == usr/sbin/ifup, exactly stock's path).
# CONFIG_IFUP is not set
# CONFIG_IFDOWN is not set

# util-linux parity (usbmount work, docs/util-linux-parity.md). Stock ships the
# real util-linux ELF versions of all of the tools below (verified against
# work/imgroot), and this build now enables BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX_BINARIES + the
# matching program toggles. Two packages that install a program at the same path
# collide, and the last install wins non-deterministically -- so, same idiom as
# ifup/ifdown above, turn OFF each BusyBox applet that util-linux now provides so
# the util-linux binary is the one that lands. Every symbol here has a confirmed
# util-linux replacement (basic set, or an explicit BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX_* toggle
# in the defconfig); applets with NO util-linux equivalent are deliberately left ON.
# GETTY is off because the serial console moves to util-linux agetty (inittab).
# CONFIG_BLKID is not set
# CONFIG_DMESG is not set
# CONFIG_FDISK is not set
# CONFIG_FLOCK is not set
# CONFIG_FSFREEZE is not set
# CONFIG_FSTRIM is not set
# CONFIG_GETOPT is not set
# CONFIG_GETTY is not set
# CONFIG_HEXDUMP is not set
# CONFIG_HWCLOCK is not set
# CONFIG_KILL is not set
# CONFIG_MKSWAP is not set
# CONFIG_MORE is not set
# CONFIG_MOUNT is not set
# CONFIG_MOUNTPOINT is not set
# CONFIG_NOLOGIN is not set
# CONFIG_READPROFILE is not set
# CONFIG_RENICE is not set
# CONFIG_SETARCH is not set
# CONFIG_SETSID is not set
# CONFIG_SWAPON is not set
# CONFIG_SWAPOFF is not set
# CONFIG_SWITCH_ROOT is not set
# CONFIG_UMOUNT is not set
# CONFIG_CHRT is not set

# T5 -- nine coreutils-style applets BusyBox 1.38.0 offers but this image had
# left off. Ten Kconfig symbols for nine commands: `netcat` is an alias name
# for the ninth (`nc`) rather than a tenth applet -- see the nc paragraph
# below. Verified, not assumed: output/build/busybox-1.38.0/.config had every
# one of the ten as "# CONFIG_X is not set" before this fragment touched it
# (re-checked 2026-07-27, including CONFIG_NETCAT). EIGHT of
# the nine are a true 1:1 reproduction: stock ships them as GNU coreutils --
# work/imgroot's usr/bin/{stat,timeout,tac,shuf,comm,split,expand,groups} are
# all symlinks -> coreutils -- and BusyBox's applets take the same names at the
# same paths, so a script that ran on stock runs here.
# `stat` and `timeout` matter most in practice: `timeout N cmd` and
# `stat -c '%s' file` are common in ordinary shell scripts, so a MiSTer script
# that runs fine on stock currently fails outright on this image.
#
# `nc` is the ONE exception and is a DELIBERATE SUBSTITUTION, not a
# reproduction. Do not let the "stock ships all nine" shorthand elsewhere in
# this repo mislead: stock's provider is a SEPARATE GNU TOOL, not BusyBox.
# work/imgroot/usr/bin/netcat is a 34376-byte ARM ELF whose strings include
# "GNU netcat %s, a rewrite of the famous networking tool." and "netcat (The
# GNU Netcat) %s" -- i.e. GNU Netcat, exactly what Buildroot's own
# package/netcat provides (netcat.mk:7, NETCAT_VERSION = 0.7.1) -- and
# usr/bin/nc is a symlink -> netcat beside it. BusyBox's nc is a different
# implementation with a different option set (see the NC_* sub-feature notes
# below), so this trades byte-for-byte parity for ~11 kB instead of a whole
# extra package. That is the intended call: `nc` on a MiSTer is for ad-hoc
# on-device network poking, not for anything that depends on GNU Netcat's
# exact flags. If a real GNU-Netcat dependency ever turns up, the fix is
# BR2_PACKAGE_NETCAT in the defconfig plus turning CONFIG_NC/CONFIG_NETCAT
# back off here -- not patching around it.
# Stock ships BOTH names (`nc` and `netcat`), and CONFIG_NC alone would only
# give the first: BusyBox has a separate alias applet for the second
# (networking/nc.c:17-21 "config NETCAT / bool 'netcat (11 kb)' / default n /
# Alias to nc.", declared at nc.c:51 as
# APPLET_ODDNAME(netcat, nc, BB_DIR_USR_BIN, ...) -> the same usr/bin/netcat
# path stock uses). It costs one more applet-table entry and one more symlink
# -- the 11 kB of code is shared with CONFIG_NC, not paid twice -- so both are
# turned on below and neither of stock's two names 404s.
# No collision with a real package: nothing in this defconfig sets
# BR2_PACKAGE_NETCAT or BR2_PACKAGE_NETCAT_OPENBSD (both exist in this
# Buildroot tree, package/netcat{,-openbsd}/, and would collide with `nc` at
# the same usr/bin/nc path exactly like the LSOF/LSUSB/MKDOSFS/{CHVT,
# DEALLOCVT,OPENVT,SETKEYCODES} block below) -- confirmed by grepping this
# file's own defconfig for both symbols, neither is present.
#
# Two of the nine gate real functionality behind their OWN sub-feature symbol,
# and the base config (package/busybox/busybox.config) pins each of those
# EXPLICITLY off -- not merely silent-by-default. An explicit "is not set" is
# a recorded choice that Kconfig's oldconfig/olddefconfig NEVER overrides with
# a symbol's own "default y" (it only fills in symbols that have no prior
# state at all) -- so turning STAT/SPLIT/NC on by themselves, without also
# turning these on, would silently ship crippled versions of all three:
#   - stat: FEATURE_STAT_FORMAT gates `-c FORMAT` and FEATURE_STAT_FILESYSTEM
#     gates `-f`. Without the first, `stat -c '%s' file` -- the single most
#     common scripted use of stat -- fails with "invalid option -- c".
#   - split: FEATURE_SPLIT_FANCY gates the 'k'/'m'/'g' suffixes on -b.
#   - nc: NC_SERVER gates `-l` (listen/server mode), NC_EXTRA gates
#     `-e`/`-i`/`-w`, and NC_110_COMPAT gates `-s`/`-n`/`-u`/`-v`/`-z` --
#     i.e. UDP mode and verbose output. All THREE were off in the base
#     config; a netcat that can neither listen nor speak UDP is not much of
#     a netcat for on-device debugging, so all three are turned on below.
CONFIG_STAT=y
CONFIG_FEATURE_STAT_FORMAT=y
CONFIG_FEATURE_STAT_FILESYSTEM=y
CONFIG_TIMEOUT=y
CONFIG_TAC=y
CONFIG_SHUF=y
CONFIG_COMM=y
CONFIG_SPLIT=y
CONFIG_FEATURE_SPLIT_FANCY=y
CONFIG_EXPAND=y
CONFIG_GROUPS=y
CONFIG_NC=y
CONFIG_NETCAT=y
CONFIG_NC_SERVER=y
CONFIG_NC_EXTRA=y
CONFIG_NC_110_COMPAT=y

# T5 -- turn OFF seven BusyBox applets that collide with real packages T5 adds
# to the defconfig (docs/package-manifest.md §4c). Same non-deterministic
# "two packages install the same path, last one wins" hazard the ifup/ifdown
# and util-linux blocks above already warn about, and the same fix: disable
# BusyBox's stub so the real package's binary is the one that lands,
# deterministically. Every collision below was found by grepping THIS
# BusyBox's actual resolved .config for the real package's installed binary
# name before the package was added -- not discovered later at boot:
#   - lsof: BR2_PACKAGE_LSOF installs $(TARGET_DIR)/usr/bin/lsof verbatim
#     (package/lsof/lsof.mk:47). BusyBox's own `lsof` applet is a 3.7 kB
#     PID/exe/file lister with none of the real lsof's network-socket, NFS,
#     `-p`, or `-i` support.
#   - lsusb: BR2_PACKAGE_USBUTILS installs usr/bin/lsusb -- confirmed against
#     stock itself: work/imgroot's usr/bin/lsusb is this same real ~150 KB
#     usbutils ELF, not a BusyBox stub. BusyBox's `lsusb` (4.4 kB) only reads
#     /sys/bus/usb/devices and cannot print vendor/product strings the way
#     scripts that parse `lsusb` output expect (no usb.ids database).
#   - mkdosfs: BR2_PACKAGE_DOSFSTOOLS_MKFS_FAT's --enable-compat-symlinks
#     install ALSO creates sbin/mkdosfs -> mkfs.fat (package/dosfstools/
#     dosfstools.mk:47, read directly, not assumed) -- the exact path
#     BusyBox's own `mkdosfs` applet uses.
#   - chvt, deallocvt, openvt, setkeycodes: BR2_PACKAGE_KBD's unconditional
#     (chvt/deallocvt/openvt) and KEYCODES_PROGS-gated (setkeycodes; "yes" on
#     every arch except m68k, kbd-2.9.0/configure.ac:143, so unconditional
#     here too) PROGS list (kbd-2.9.0/src/Makefile.am:6-11) installs all four
#     at $(bindir) == usr/bin/, the same path BusyBox's applets use. Checked
#     against Main_MiSTer too, since chvt sounds Main-relevant: its
#     video_chvt() (work/Main_MiSTer/video.cpp) is a C function that calls the
#     VT ioctls directly, never popen()s the `chvt` binary -- so whichever
#     provider wins here has zero effect on Main itself, only on interactive
#     or script use of these four names.
#   Considered and ruled OUT as non-collisions (so deliberately NOT listed
#   below -- there is nothing to turn off). This enumeration is meant to be
#   the COMPLETE complement of the list above, and it was derived mechanically
#   rather than by eye: every name kbd and dosfstools install was intersected
#   against every APPLET*() name declared by a `//applet:` line in
#   output/build/busybox-1.38.0 (433 of them, 2026-07-27) --
#     grep -rho '^//applet:.*' output/build/busybox-1.38.0/*/*.c \
#       output/build/busybox-1.38.0/*/*/*.c \
#       | grep -oP '(?<=APPLET\()[a-z0-9_.]+|(?<=APPLET_ODDNAME\()[a-z0-9_.]+|(?<=APPLET_NOEXEC\()[a-z0-9_.]+|(?<=APPLET_NOFORK\()[a-z0-9_.]+' \
#       | sort -u
#   -- rather than from memory. Redo it that way, not from this list, if
#   either package's version moves.
#     (a) No BusyBox applet of that name exists AT ALL: dosfstools'
#         `dosfslabel`, `fsck.vfat`, `fsck.msdos`, `dosfsck`, `mkfs.msdos`,
#         and kbd's `getkeycodes`, `dumpkeys`, `loadkeys`, `showconsolefont`,
#         `setleds`, `setmetamode`, `psfxtable` (+ its `psfaddtable`/
#         `psfgettable`/`psfstriptable` symlinks, src/Makefile.am:79-82),
#         `kbdrate`, `kbdinfo`, `setvtrgb`, `mapscrn`, `loadunimap`,
#         `unicode_start`, `unicode_stop`. Nothing to resolve. (kbd's
#         OPTIONAL_PROGS set -- screendump, setpalette, spawn_login, ... -- and
#         `resizecons` are not in scope: the first defaults to "no"
#         (configure.ac:194-198, and kbd.mk passes no --enable-optional-progs),
#         the second is x86-only (configure.ac:148-150), so neither is built
#         for this target at all.)
#     (b) Same name, same path, but the applet is pinned off in the base
#         config so the collision is latent, not live -- ONE Kconfig flip from
#         becoming real, which is why they are written down instead of just
#         omitted. BusyBox's `mkfs.vfat` alias (CONFIG_MKFS_VFAT,
#         busybox.config:669) vs dosfstools' compat symlink; and three kbd
#         programs, all of them bin_PROGRAMS -> $(bindir) == usr/bin/ via
#         kbd-2.9.0/src/Makefile.am:7-8, against applets that land at exactly
#         the same path:
#           - showkey    console-tools/showkey.c:15, BB_DIR_USR_BIN
#                        (busybox.config:396, "# CONFIG_SHOWKEY is not set")
#           - fgconsole  console-tools/fgconsole.c:15, BB_DIR_USR_BIN
#                        (busybox.config:375)
#           - kbd_mode   console-tools/kbd_mode.c:17, BB_DIR_BIN -- which IS
#                        usr/bin here: BR2_ROOTFS_MERGED_USR=y makes
#                        target/bin a symlink to usr/bin (busybox.config:376)
#     (c) Same name, DIFFERENT path, so it can never collide: BusyBox's
#         `setfont` is BB_DIR_USR_SBIN (console-tools/loadfont.c:53) ==
#         /usr/sbin/setfont, while kbd's is /usr/bin/setfont. Worth stating
#         explicitly because the guarded inittab line calls the absolute path
#         /usr/bin/setfont (rootfs-overlay/etc/inittab:73) -- i.e. it asks for
#         kbd's by construction and could not accidentally get BusyBox's.
#         Stock agrees: work/imgroot has usr/bin/setfont and no
#         usr/sbin/setfont. (CONFIG_SETFONT is off in the base config anyway,
#         busybox.config:378, so today the name exists only once.)
#     (d) `vlock`: BusyBox's is ON in the base config (busybox.config:561,
#         CONFIG_VLOCK=y) and owns the name uncontested, because Buildroot
#         builds kbd with `--disable-vlock` (kbd.mk:11) -- kbd's own vlock is
#         never compiled, let alone installed. Nothing to turn off.
# CONFIG_LSOF is not set
# CONFIG_LSUSB is not set
# CONFIG_MKDOSFS is not set
# CONFIG_CHVT is not set
# CONFIG_DEALLOCVT is not set
# CONFIG_OPENVT is not set
# CONFIG_SETKEYCODES is not set

# GNU wget vs BusyBox's wget applet -- issue #130 (2026-09-01). Same
# "two packages install the same path, last one wins" hazard as the ifup/ifdown,
# util-linux and lsof/lsusb/mkdosfs/kbd blocks above, and the same fix.
# BR2_PACKAGE_WGET (added to the defconfig in the same change) installs a real
# GNU wget 1.25.0 at usr/bin/wget; BusyBox's CONFIG_WGET applet -- ON in the base
# config (output/build/busybox-1.38.0/.config:1003, read directly) -- claims the
# identical path via busybox.links:217. Turning the applet off makes the GNU
# binary the deterministic winner.
#
# This one is a functional fix, not just determinism. BusyBox's applet was built
# with BOTH of its TLS knobs off -- "# CONFIG_FEATURE_WGET_HTTPS is not set"
# (.config:1009) and "# CONFIG_FEATURE_WGET_OPENSSL is not set" (.config:1010) --
# which makes SSL_SUPPORTED 0, so networking/wget.c's scheme dispatch has no
# https:// arm at all (the P_HTTPS branch at wget.c:568-572 is inside
# `#if SSL_SUPPORTED`) and every https URL falls through to
# bb_error_msg_and_die("not an http or ftp url: %s") at wget.c:578. That is the
# verbatim error in issue #130.
#
# Considered and rejected: flipping CONFIG_FEATURE_WGET_HTTPS=y here instead of
# adding the package. It is the cheaper edit but the wrong one. BusyBox's own
# Kconfig help for that symbol says its internal TLS "does *not* check that the
# peer is who it claims to be", does not verify signature hashes on incoming
# data, and does not check the peer's certificate -- i.e. it would give the
# MiSTer an https:// that silently accepts an impersonating server, on a box
# whose whole job is downloading cores and updates over the network. It would
# also still ignore /etc/wgetrc and still not match stock's option set. Stock
# solved this by shipping GNU wget; so do we.
#
# Divergence from stock, stated rather than glossed: stock kept CONFIG_WGET=y in
# its BusyBox *and* shipped the GNU ELF, so `busybox wget` remained reachable
# there as a hand-built-image accident. Buildroot cannot express that safely --
# two packages owning usr/bin/wget is exactly the non-deterministic install-order
# race this file exists to prevent -- so the applet goes off and `busybox wget`
# is gone. Nothing calls it by that spelling; the `wget` name, which is what
# scripts use, is answered by a strictly more capable binary.
# CONFIG_WGET is not set
